---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Command from '../components/Command.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import download from '../data/downloads.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const windows = 'python -m venv .venv\n.\\.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1\npython -m pip install -e .';
const unix = 'python3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install -e .';
---
<Page title="Set up FreeCompute — V1 CLI guide" description="Install the current FreeCompute V1 source preview, start its dual-T4 Kaggle notebook, configure your private key, and connect your local CLI.">
  <article class="guide container">
    <a class="text-link guide-back" href={base}>← Back to FreeCompute</a>
    <header class="guide-header"><p class="eyebrow">V{download.version} beta · Source preview</p><h1>From notebook<br />to your terminal.</h1><p>Start with the documented Kaggle text worker. You need Python 3.10+, a Kaggle account with access to the required GPUs, and a project on your PC.</p></header>
    <nav class="guide-toc" aria-label="Setup guide sections"><a href="#install">01 Install</a><a href="#worker">02 Worker</a><a href="#connect">03 Connect</a><a href="#safety">04 Safety</a></nav>
    <section id="install" aria-labelledby="install-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 / Local setup</p><h2 id="install-title">Install the current CLI.</h2>
      <p><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}downloads/freecompute-v1-source.zip`} download>Download the V1 source preview</a>, extract it, and open a terminal inside <code>FreeCompute-v1-preview</code>. This bundle contains the tracked CLI source from commit <code>{download.commit.slice(0, 7)}</code>. It is a beta source snapshot, not a published binary release.</p>
      <p>The GitHub default branch may differ from this preview. Use the bundle for the behavior and commands shown here.</p>
      <Command label="Windows PowerShell installation" command={windows} />
      <Command label="macOS / Linux installation" command={unix} />
      <p>If PowerShell blocks activation, use the virtual environment executables directly: <code>.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install -e .</code>, then <code>.\.venv\Scripts\freecompute.exe</code>.</p>
      <p>From the extracted directory, copy <code>harness/config.sample.yaml</code> to a private <code>config.yaml</code>. Its Kaggle profile matches the supplied notebook. Create a private <code>.env</code> beside it with <code>FREECOMPUTE_API_KEY</code> set to your own nonempty bearer key. Use that same value in Kaggle Secrets. Keep the key out of commands and screenshots.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="worker" aria-labelledby="worker-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">02 / Remote setup</p><h2 id="worker-title">Start the Kaggle worker.</h2>
      <p><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}downloads/freecompute_dual_gpu_server.ipynb`} download>Download the current worker notebook <Icon name="arrow" /></a>. This is an unchanged copy of <code>kaggle/freecompute_dual_gpu_server.ipynb</code> in the source preview. Upload it into the Kaggle notebook editor.</p>
      <ol class="guide-list">
        <li>Set the accelerator to <strong>GPU T4 x2</strong>, <strong>Internet ON</strong>, and <strong>Persistence None</strong>. If those GPUs are unavailable for your account, this preset cannot run as documented.</li>
        <li>Enable the notebook’s private Kaggle Secret named <code>FREECOMPUTE_API_KEY</code>, matching your local <code>.env</code>.</li>
        <li>No dataset input is required. Your own verified private dataset from <code>kaggle/dataset_builder.ipynb</code> is optional. A cold setup may need to build the pinned engine and download the model; no fixed startup time is promised.</li>
        <li>Run executable cells <strong>1 through 8</strong> in heading order. Stop on errors. For a fresh session, Run All is safe because cell 9 skips shutdown by default. Avoid Save & Run All for this interactive workflow.</li>
        <li>Cell 6 should print <code>SUPERVISOR HEALTHY</code>. Copy the temporary <strong>Remote URL</strong> from cell 7. Cell 8 should print <code>WORKER READY FOR LOCAL ACCEPTANCE</code>. A readiness marker alone does not prove inference.</li>
        <li>Keep Kaggle running while using the CLI. Do not rerun setup, transport cells or Run All during local work.</li>
      </ol>
      <aside class="guide-note"><strong>Choose a transport that carries your responses.</strong><p>Cloudflare Quick Tunnels <a class="inline-link" href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/tunnel/get-started/quick-tunnels/">do not officially support SSE</a>. A historical FreeCompute session streamed through one, but your route may buffer or fail. Use a supported private route or a suitable named tunnel when needed. Tailscale mode additionally needs its auth secret and a client on your PC. HTTPS encrypts transit; plain remote HTTP does not.</p></aside>
    </section>
    <section id="connect" aria-labelledby="connect-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">03 / Connect and work</p><h2 id="connect-title">Use the fresh URL.</h2>
      <p>In the activated environment, from the source directory containing your private config and key:</p>
      <Command label="Connect your text worker" command={'freecompute --remote-url "PASTE-KAGGLE-URL-HERE"'} />
      <p>Replace the placeholder with cell 7’s full URL. The flag overrides a stale URL in <code>config.yaml</code> for this run. The key comes from the existing environment/config mechanism and is never part of the command.</p>
      <p>Select an existing project with <code>--workspace</code>:</p>
      <Command label="Choose a Windows project" command={'freecompute --workspace "C:\\path\\to\\project" --remote-url "PASTE-KAGGLE-URL-HERE"'} />
      <p>Use your actual project path; on Linux/macOS use the corresponding local path. At the prompt, type <code>/</code> for commands, <code>/status</code> for worker observations, or ask it to inspect a small project. Review proposed edits and commands before approving with <code>y</code>. Blank input denies approval.</p>
      <h3>If the tunnel changes</h3><Command label="Reconnect inside FreeCompute" command="/connect PASTE-KAGGLE-URL-HERE" />
      <p>Reconnect uses your existing key, checks authenticated health/models, and changes the active endpoint in memory. It does not save the URL or resubmit completed tasks. Active or uncertain inference leases can block reconnect; do not clear them based only on healthy status or low GPU usage.</p>
      <h3>When finished</h3><p>Exit the CLI after local work ends. Explicitly enable <strong>MANUAL SHUTDOWN</strong> in notebook cell 9, run it, then click Kaggle <strong>Stop Session</strong>. Saved sessions are available through <code>/sessions</code> and <code>/resume ID</code>.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="safety" aria-labelledby="safety-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">04 / Read before approving</p><h2 id="safety-title">You control local execution.</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list"><li>The local Core validates tool arguments, obtains approval, runs tools and records receipts. The inference worker proposes actions; it has no direct local filesystem or terminal endpoint.</li><li>Prompts, selected file contents and tool results reach the inference host. Avoid secrets in prompts. Transport encryption does not hide model input from the host.</li><li>File tools enforce workspace and protected-path boundaries. An approved shell command runs with your user privileges, not in an OS sandbox.</li><li>Socket closure is not remote cancellation acknowledgement. Unknown outcomes keep capacity held until explicit reconciliation with independent evidence.</li><li>65,536 tokens is the preset’s declared allocation. Prior live context testing reached 9,714 actual input tokens; full 65,536-token input is not certified. Model size, quantization and hardware affect practical capacity.</li></ul>
      <p>Colab and local/private deployments require manual compatible worker/profile configuration. Image generation uses a separate fixed ComfyUI workflow and its own authenticated gateway/key; a maintained authenticated image notebook is not supplied. No provider quota, fixed session duration or universal model compatibility is guaranteed.</p>
    </section>
    <div class="guide-provenance"><h3>Source and integrity</h3><p>The preview includes the README, security policy, knowledgebase, unit tests, skills and current worker files. Build provenance and hashes describe this snapshot; they are not an independent signature or a production certification.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${base}downloads/SHA256SUMS.txt`}>Download checksums <Icon name="arrow" /></a><a class="text-link" href={`${base}downloads/provenance.json`}>Source provenance <Icon name="arrow" /></a></div>
  </article>
  <script>import '../scripts/interactions';</script>
</Page>
